              "id": "glog-44006f16-4a31-441b-b8bc-c07c626b3220",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The \"Unsecured Web Address\" vulnerability refers to the use of unencrypted HTTP protocol to access or transfer data over the internet. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks, where an attacker can intercept and potentially alter the communication between two parties without their knowledge.\n\nIn the context of C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a program uses libraries or functions that perform network operations over HTTP, instead of secure HTTPS.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for network communication. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept or alter the data.\n\nIf you are using a library that does not support HTTPS, consider switching to a different library that does. If this is not possible, you may need to implement your own encryption on top of the HTTP communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink you provided is a URL, not a piece of C++ code. However, if you were using a library like libcurl to access this URL in your C++ program, you could switch to using HTTPS like so:\n\n```cpp\n#include <curl/curl.h>\n\nint main() {\n    CURL *curl = curl_easy_init();\n    if(curl) {\n        CURLcode res;\n        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"https://hpcg-benchmark.org\");\n        res = curl_easy_perform(curl);\n        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the libcurl library.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
